::PHY-U1-PRE-Q01::A runner moves 60 m east in 12 s. What is the runner's average velocity? {=5 m/s east ~5 m/s west ~72 m/s east ~0.20 m/s east}
::PHY-U1-PRE-Q02::Which graph represents constant positive velocity? {~A horizontal position-time line =A straight position-time line with positive slope ~A curved position-time line ~A horizontal velocity-time line at zero}
::PHY-U1-PRE-Q03::A car changes velocity from 10 m/s to 22 m/s in 4 s. What is its average acceleration? {~8 m/s² ~5.5 m/s² =3 m/s² ~48 m/s²}
::PHY-U1-PRE-Q04::At the highest point of a projectile's path, which statement is correct when air resistance is ignored? {~Both velocity components are zero ~Horizontal velocity is zero ~Acceleration is zero =Vertical velocity is zero while acceleration is downward}
::PHY-U1-PRE-Q05::According to Newton's second law, what happens to acceleration when net force doubles and mass stays constant? {=Acceleration doubles ~Acceleration is halved ~Acceleration stays constant ~Acceleration becomes zero}
::PHY-U1-PRE-Q06::Which force acts perpendicular to a surface and supports an object resting on it? {~Friction =Normal force ~Tension ~Weight}
::PHY-U1-PRE-Q07::A book rests motionless on a table. Which best describes the forces on the book? {~No forces act ~Only gravity acts =The upward normal force balances the downward weight ~Friction balances gravity}
::PHY-U1-PRE-Q08::Two horizontal forces, 18 N right and 6 N left, act on a 4 kg object. What is its acceleration? {~6 m/s² right ~3 m/s² left ~4.5 m/s² right =3 m/s² right}
::PHY-U1-PRE-Q09::Pressure is calculated by dividing force by which quantity? {=Area ~Mass ~Time ~Distance}
::PHY-U1-PRE-Q10::Which process can raise Earth's crust toward gravitational equilibrium after erosion removes mass? {~Mechanical weathering =Isostatic uplift ~Shear failure ~Projectile motion}

